[Music]  there's a lot of kind of discussion of  what the political beliefs of Silicon  Valley elites and founders are but we  actually have no hard data on what they  actually believe when it comes to  politics and policy  some people say Silicon Valley is really  libertarian other people say Silicon  Valley they're all just like loyal  Democrats Silicon Valley you know they  all just care about these social issues  and they're very far left on social  issues however although there's been a  lot of thinking about for example people  on Wall Street how do they think about  politics what do they want how do they  interface with the political system  there have been much less thinking about  that same question in the context of  Silicon Valley  technology elites are basically liberal  on every dimension you could think of  except regulation and this is actually  really interesting because there's no  other group of people that we surveyed  that have this constellation of  political beliefs and specifically when  it looked you come to economic issues  they are not easily categorized as  libertarians  because they believe in the  redistribution of income for things like  health care and education and poverty  reduction but at the same time they  believe that government should not be  regulating business and some people have  now called this new type of political  animal the liberal terian so they're  trying to find new words and  terminologies and so it's been kind of  interesting to see how people have tried  to introduce new categories to match the  results we've been finding  a lot of Silicon Valley companies right  now are intervening in labor markets  uber is a great example where there's  the question of whether gig workers for  uber should be treated like regular  workers or as contractors you can  imagine labor unions see that as a  classic case where it's important these  workers receive protections all the  protections that would be associated  with traditional employees whereas  technology entrepreneurs look at this  and they say no we shouldn't be  regulating how these companies um treat  workers we should give them freedom in  the marketplace and so that's a classic  example where I think in a lot of states  that issues starting to come to a head  and you could imagine it's the sort of  thing that those who work for tech  companies who are doing more donating to  Democratic campaigns are gonna say  actually we disagree with the labor  unions that are endorsing you we think  you should take this other position on  this issue so those kinds of tensions  within the Democratic Party we think are  going to become increasingly common  a lot of historical research is found  that parties usually change when there's  important groups within those parties  that agree with them on most things but  disagree with them on a few things and  then they shape the party in that  direction so a classic example is at the  afl-cio the CIO portion of it before it  merged was very tolerant of racial  progress and at the time the Democrats  had a lot of virulent racist people as  leaders in that party and they sort of  used labor which they agreed with the  Democratic Party on and shifted the  Democratic Party to being more of a pro  civil rights party and this pattern has  emerged over and over again in American  history  so one prediction our research suggests  is that as technology elites become more  important part of the Democratic Party  in terms of his fundraising apparatus  they are going to double down and  promote traditional democratic interests  when it comes to Social Policy  redistribution foreign policy  immigration but try to move it towards  the right on issues when it comes to  regulation particularly of labor markets  I think a big question right now in  politics is the gap between elites and  masses and for the whole post-world War  II period there's been sort of an  agreement that we would devolve some  power to elites who would then look out  for like everybody's interests but  increasingly we've seen populist revolts  going on in the United States and all  the way across the world where people in  the masses have kind of realized our  wages have stagnated expenses and real  terms have gone up on things like health  insurance and college tuition etc and  our children are going to be living  worse lives with the worst standard of  living than we did and so there's a lot  of discussion about well how is our  democratic institutions gonna survive if  there's not going to be cooperation  between masses and elites so I think a  lot of this research has to continue to  explore this question more I think David  and I are just generally interested in  kind of understanding well who are the  elites and what does that mean and  what's they believe and before we did a  study like this there really was no data  and it's because these are very hard  people to interview and get their  opinions on and I think just future work  needs to be done on this topic so we can  understand the contours and potential  fault lines in politics both in the  United States and abroad the other  transformation you've seen economically  for a lot of Americans is not only have  we - stagnated but people are exposed to  increasing risk and so a lot of the kind  of government-run programs that  technology entrepreneurs can be more  skeptical of they're not just about  transferring resources they're about  some form of social insurance and so I  think as people are exposed to more and  more risk that might be an experience  that technology entrepreneurs haven't  directly experienced as much and so  that's another gap that I'm interested  in in looking at I'm a big believer that  I think social science should be  addressing questions that are important  to the broader world that if the world  says these are the issues that are  important to how human beings are gonna  have to grapple with their democracies  their societies so that's what academics  should be studying and so that's one  reason I was very proud of this study is  that these issues of inequality  automation technology this is  what the world is facing right now and  so I think it's important to address  questions that are relevant  [Music]  you

